# Line endings: the repository stores LF, and a Windows working copy may use CRLF
# (core.autocrlf). Shell scripts are pinned to LF in both, because a CRLF script fails
# to run under bash on Linux CI.

*.sh text eol=lf

# Binary assets: never normalise.
*.png binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.dll binary
*.pdb binary
*.nupkg binary
*.snupkg binary

# The upstream declarations the build reads are JSON, compared as text, so the endings are pinned
# to keep a Windows working copy from disagreeing with a Linux one. This is stated first because the
# vendored trees below live under the same directory and must not inherit it: the last matching
# pattern wins.
src/upstream/** text eol=lf

# The vendored frontend is upstream's compiled bundle, checked in byte for byte (ADR-0002).
# Text normalisation would rewrite line endings on checkout and change the tree that is
# embedded in the assembly, so it is excluded from it entirely. GitHub is told not to count
# it as hand-written source.
src/upstream/frontend/** -text linguist-vendored

# The same for the vendored SSR runtime, which is upstream's compiled SvelteKit server.
src/upstream/ssr/** -text linguist-vendored

# And for the curated workflow template list, which is upstream's own data served to the canvas.
# It is text, so this is the one tree where the rule above matters on every checkout rather than
# only for compiled bytes.
src/upstream/workflow/** -text linguist-vendored

# The sample media the five helpers hand a handler. Most of it is binary, but the tree also
# carries text - a subtitle file, a plain-text sample - and a checkout that rewrote those would
# change the bytes the helpers serve without changing a byte a reader can see.
src/upstream/media/** -text linguist-vendored
